.TH TPM2SH 1 "2026-10-10" "tpm2sh 1.2.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tpm2sh \- a command-line tool for accessing TPM 2.0 devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tpm2sh
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fICOMMAND-OPTIONS\fR] [\fIARGUMENTS\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tpm2sh
is a command-line tool for accessing TPM 2.0 devices.
.P
.B tpm2sh
provides a virtual memory manager for sharing transient TPM
resources across multiple commands.
.P
Transient handles are cached in a per-user cache directory. The cache
automatically invalidates and resets on reboot, and does not require
attention from the user.
.P
.B tpm2sh
provides subcommands for creating and loading PEM encoded keys in TPMKey
ASN.1 format.
.P
Several commands, such as
.BR create ,
.BR create-primary ,
.BR import ,
and
.BR seal ,
accept a
.BR --policy " option to specify an inline authorization policy expression."
.P
RSA and ECC algorithm strings use the form
.IR type-selector:hash[:scheme] .
Omitting the scheme suffix describes a restricted storage parent
(\fBrsa-2048:sha256\fR, \fBecc-nist-p256:sha256\fR).
A \fB:null\fR suffix describes an unrestricted signing and decrypt key.
A bound scheme (\fB:rsassa\fR, \fB:ecdsa\fR, \fB:oaep\fR, \fB:ecdh\fR, and so on)
selects that scheme and the matching usage bits.
The
.B algorithm
command lists the forms supported by the attached TPM.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -d, --device=\fI<DEVICE>\fR
Device file [default: /dev/tpmrm0].
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print help.
.TP
.B -V, --version
Print version.

.SH INPUT/OUTPUT FORMATTING
.B tpm2sh
uses the \fBPEM\fR format for all key input and output operations.
.TP
.B Stdin/Stdout
If \fI--input\fR is not specified, input is read from stdin as \fBPEM\fR.
If \fI--output\fR is not specified, output is written to stdout as \fBPEM\fR.
.TP
.B Files
When reading from or writing to files, data is treated as \fBPEM\fR formatted text.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B algorithm
Lists available algorithms supported by the chip.
.TP
.B cache
Lists cached TPM objects.
.TP
.B create
Creates a secondary key or a sealed data object.
.TP
.B create-primary
Creates a new primary key in a specified hierarchy.
.TP
.B delete
Deletes active and cached objects.
.TP
.B evict
Create persistent object from transient object.
.TP
.B help
Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s).
.TP
.B import
Import an external PEM private key as a TPMKey. The optional algorithm
argument selects the public-area form; when omitted, an unrestricted
.B :null
key of the imported type and size is used.
.TP
.B load
Loads a PEM TPMKey file to cache. If the TPMKey file does not include
a parent public area, a parent handle must be supplied with
.BR -P " or " --parent .
.TP
.B memory
Lists objects inside TPM memory or inspects a single handle.
.TP
.B pcr-event
Extends a PCR with an event.
.TP
.B reset-lock
Resets the dictionary attack lockout counter.
.TP
.B return-code
Prints a TPM return code in human-readable format.
.TP
.B unseal
Retrieves data from a sealed data object.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TPM2SH_CACHE_PATH
Overrides the default per-user cache directory used to store virtual TPM
state. When unset, a system-appropriate per-user cache directory is used.
.TP
.B TPM2SH_AUTH
A comma-separated list of handle authentication values in the format
<handle>:<hex>. This allows providing passwords without exposing them in the
process argument list. Values provided via \-\-auth take precedence.

